      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Yes, Thompson's WaterSeal works as a water repellent: on clean, dry wood, the clear versions make water bead and run off, and they slow the swelling and cracking that come from rain soaking into the boards. What the clear formulas do not do is stop sun damage, so the wood keeps graying underneath, and on a sunny deck floor you should expect to reapply about once a year rather than every few years. That makes it a reasonable budget choice for some jobs and a poor one for others. On a new pressure-treated fence, a shaded deck or a project where you are happy to recoat every spring, a clear water repellent is cheap, easy and fine. On a sunny deck where you want to keep the wood's color and avoid annual work, a tinted product or a penetrating oil sealer is usually the better buy. This guide explains what Thompson's actually does, how the different product lines differ, how to make it last, and when to buy something else.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="what-it-does" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What Does Thompson's Water Seal Actually Do?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Thompson's WaterSeal is one of the best-known names in wood protection, and its core product is a clear water repellent. A water repellent soaks into the top layer of the wood and leaves behind ingredients, such as waxes, silicones or resins depending on the formula, that make the surface shed water instead of absorbing it. Right after application, water beads up on the boards in a way that is genuinely impressive, and that is the effect the brand is built around.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keeping water out matters because water is what drives most of the damage you can see on a wood deck. Boards that soak up rain swell, then shrink as they dry, and that cycle opens checks and cracks, loosens fasteners and encourages cupping and warping. A water repellent slows how much water gets in, so the wood moves less and stays more stable. On pressure-treated lumber, which is already protected against rot and insects, reducing that movement is most of what a clear finish can offer.
          </p>

          <p>
            What a clear water repellent does not do is form a protective film or add color. It is not a paint and not a stain. It does not hide gray wood, stop UV light, fill cracks or create a hard surface that resists scuffs. If you put it on a deck that is already gray and splintered, the deck will shed water but still look gray and splintered. Understanding that limitation is the key to whether you will be happy with it.
          </p>

          <p>
            So the honest short answer is yes, it works, as long as you judge it as a clear water repellent. The complaints you see online usually come from people who expected it to behave like a multi-year deck stain, or who applied it to new, wet or dirty wood where it could not soak in. Our <a href="/deck-stain-vs-sealer/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">deck stain vs sealer</a> guide explains the difference between these product categories.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="graying" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Why Does Wood Still Turn Gray Under Thompson's?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Wood turns gray because of sunlight, not water. Ultraviolet light breaks down lignin, the natural compound that binds wood fibers together, in the top layer of the board. Rain then washes those loose fibers away, and what is left looks silver or gray. A clear finish lets that UV light straight through to the wood, so even a perfectly applied clear water repellent cannot stop the graying process. It only slows the water half of the problem.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pigment is the only practical UV blocker in a deck finish. The fine colored particles in a toner, semi-transparent stain or solid stain absorb or reflect sunlight before it reaches the wood. That is why tinted products keep a deck looking like wood for longer, and why the clear versions of almost every brand, not just Thompson's, gray out faster. Some clear products include UV absorbers, which help a little, but they do not match pigment.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE KEY POINT</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Clear keeps water out. Only pigment keeps sun out.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If keeping the wood's color matters to you, the clear product is the wrong tool, whatever the brand. Look for a toner or semi-transparent formula instead.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Graying is not just cosmetic. The weathered fibers on the surface are weak and loose, so they shed the next coat of finish along with them. That is part of why clear repellents wear out quickly on sunny decks: the wood surface they are bonded to is slowly breaking down underneath. Cleaning with a deck cleaner and brightener before each recoat removes those loose fibers and gives the new coat a sound surface to soak into.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you like the silver look, none of this is a problem. Plenty of people prefer a weathered gray deck, and a clear water repellent on top of gray wood keeps it from absorbing water and growing as much mildew. The issue only comes up when buyers expect a clear finish to keep new wood looking new. It will not, and no clear product can promise that honestly.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-long" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. How Long Does Thompson's Water Seal Last on a Deck?
          </h2>

          <p>
            On a horizontal deck surface in full sun, plan on reapplying a clear water repellent about once a year. That is the typical life for the category as a whole, and it is consistent with how most clear products are positioned. Vertical surfaces like fences, railings and siding last longer because water runs off and the sun hits them at an angle, so they can often go longer between coats. Shaded decks also tend to last longer.
          </p>

          <p>
            Thompson's sells several product lines with different claimed wear lives, and those claims are printed on the label and on the brand's website. The longer-lasting lines are generally the tinted and stain versions, while the basic clear water repellent is positioned at the short end. Read the specific label rather than relying on a general number, and keep in mind that label claims assume good prep and normal conditions, which a sunny, busy deck may not provide.
          </p>

          <p>
            The simplest way to know when to reapply is the water test. Sprinkle a little water on several boards, especially the sunniest ones and the main walking path. If it beads up, the repellent is still working. If it soaks in and darkens the wood within a few minutes, it is time for another coat. Many deck owners find the traffic lanes fail long before the rest of the deck, and spot-treating those areas is perfectly reasonable.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not let a short life scare you off if the product suits the job. Recoating a clear water repellent is about the easiest deck finish job there is: clean, let dry, apply one coat. There is no stripping and usually no sanding, because nothing builds up on the surface. For some people, a cheap annual coat is genuinely less hassle than a more expensive multi-year product that needs more careful prep. Our guide on <a href="/how-often-should-you-seal-a-deck/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">how often to seal a deck</a> compares the schedules side by side.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="product-lines" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Which Thompson's Product Should You Buy?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Thompson's is a brand, not a single product, and the line has changed over the years. It typically includes a basic clear waterproofing wood protector, an advanced or upgraded clear version, tinted versions that add a light color, and a range of waterproofing stains in transparent, semi-transparent and solid opacities. The brand has also sold a premium Signature Series line positioned as a longer-lasting combined stain and sealer. Names and formulas change, so check what is on the shelf.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Product type</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What it is</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Best fit</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Clear water repellent</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Clear, penetrating, no pigment</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">New fences, vertical surfaces, budget yearly maintenance</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Tinted water repellent or toner</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Light pigment for some UV protection</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Decks where you want a hint of color and slightly longer life</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Transparent or semi-transparent stain</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">More pigment, still shows grain</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sunny decks where you want color and less frequent recoats</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Solid stain</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Opaque, hides grain</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Older, weathered decks with mismatched boards</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The general rule applies here just as it does for any brand: the more pigment, the more UV protection and the longer it tends to last between coats. If you are buying Thompson's for a deck floor in the sun, the tinted or semi-transparent versions are usually the better choice over the basic clear one. The clear products make the most sense on vertical surfaces and shaded areas, where UV is less of a factor.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pay attention to whether a product is water-based or oil-based, and what it says about application over existing finishes. Mixing incompatible products is a common cause of peeling and blotchy results. If your deck has an old film-forming stain on it, a penetrating water repellent may not soak in at all. The label lists what it can go over and the prep it needs, and following it matters more than the brand name.
          </p>

          <p>
            Also check the coverage rate on the can and buy accordingly. Penetrating products cover less on rough, weathered or very dry wood, because the wood soaks up more. A product that claims a few hundred square feet per gallon on smooth, new wood may cover noticeably less on an old deck. Running out halfway through a board is how lap marks happen, so buy a little extra.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="applying" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How Do You Get Thompson's Water Seal to Last Longer?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most failures of clear water repellents come from prep, not from the product. Start with a clean deck. Sweep it, then wash it with a deck cleaner to remove dirt, mildew and gray fibers, and rinse thoroughly. Our <a href="/how-to-clean-a-deck/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">guide to cleaning a deck</a> covers the steps. A repellent applied over dirt or mildew bonds to the grime instead of the wood and washes away with it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then let the wood dry completely, typically 24 to 48 hours of dry weather after cleaning or rain. Penetrating products need dry, open wood to soak into. On new pressure-treated lumber, that can mean waiting weeks or months for the wood to dry out enough to absorb anything. The water test tells you when it is ready: if a sprinkle of water soaks in within a few minutes, you can seal.
          </p>

          <p>
            Apply in mild weather, typically between about 50 and 90 degrees, out of direct midday sun, and with no rain forecast for at least a day. Use a pump sprayer, roller or brush as the label recommends, and back-brush to work it into the grain. Apply one even coat at the label's coverage rate. More is not better: excess product that sits on the surface can stay tacky and look shiny or blotchy.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, keep up with it. Because clear water repellents are short-lived, the difference between a deck that looks good and one that looks neglected is usually whether the owner recoats on time. Run the water test every spring. Recoat the high-traffic and sunny areas when they start absorbing water, and the rest of the deck when it follows. Our guide on <a href="/how-long-does-deck-sealer-take-to-dry/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">how long deck sealer takes to dry</a> helps you plan the timing around weather.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="better-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. When Is a Different Sealer the Better Buy?
          </h2>

          <p>
            If your deck sits in full sun and you want to keep its color, a clear water repellent is the wrong tool. A toner or semi-transparent sealer-stain, from Thompson's or anyone else, will block much more UV and typically last longer between coats. You give up some of the bare-wood look, but you get a deck that stays brown instead of turning silver, and you recoat less often.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you want maximum time between coats on a horizontal surface, consider a quality penetrating oil sealer. Many deck owners find oil-based penetrating products soak deeper into the wood and wear more gradually, which makes maintenance coats easy because there is no film to peel. They tend to cost more per gallon, but when you divide the cost over the years between coats, the gap narrows. Our roundup of the <a href="/best-deck-sealant/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best deck sealants</a> compares the options we think are worth buying.
          </p>

          <p>
            If your deck is old, weathered and patchy, a clear product will do little for its appearance. A semi-transparent or solid stain hides gray wood and mismatched boards much better. That is especially true after board replacements, where new boards stand out sharply against old ones. Clear sealers highlight those differences, while pigmented stains help blend them.
          </p>

          <p>
            And if the real goal is lower total effort, add up the work as well as the price. A cheap clear repellent applied every year means a cleaning and coating day every year. A better penetrating or semi-transparent product applied every two to three years means fewer of those days. For many deck owners, the time saved is worth more than the difference in price per gallon. Our breakdown of <a href="/how-much-does-it-cost-to-seal-a-deck/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">what it costs to seal a deck</a> runs the numbers over ten years.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. So, Is Thompson's Water Seal Worth Buying?
          </h2>

          <p>
            For the right job, yes. A clear Thompson's water repellent is a sensible, inexpensive choice for new pressure-treated fences, vertical surfaces, outbuildings, a shaded deck or a project where you are happy to recoat every year. It is easy to apply, widely available and does what it says: it keeps water from soaking into the wood while it lasts. On those jobs, paying more for a premium product may not buy you much.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a sunny deck floor where you want color retention and fewer recoats, the clear version is not worth it. You will be back out with the sprayer every year, and the wood will still gray. In that situation, step up to a tinted or semi-transparent formula at minimum, or a quality penetrating oil sealer. That is not a knock on the brand specifically. It is the same advice we would give for any clear water repellent.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a brand-new pressure-treated deck, a clear water repellent can be a reasonable first-year step while you decide on a long-term finish, as long as the wood is dry enough to absorb it. Our guide on <a href="/should-you-seal-pressure-treated-wood/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">whether to seal pressure-treated wood</a> covers the timing. Once the wood has weathered in, many owners move to a pigmented product for longer protection.
          </p>

          <p>
            The one thing to avoid is judging any sealer by how dramatically water beads in the first week. Almost every fresh water repellent makes water bead. What matters is how long it keeps doing that on your sunniest, busiest boards, and whether it protects against the sun as well as the rain. On those two measures, clear repellents trail pigmented products, and the price on the can should be weighed against how often you are willing to repeat the job.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting clear to stop graying</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A clear water repellent keeps water out but lets UV light straight through, so the wood keeps turning gray underneath. That is true of every clear product, not just Thompson's. If you want to keep the wood's color, buy a tinted toner or semi-transparent formula instead of the clear version.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Applying it to wet new lumber</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Fresh pressure-treated lumber is often too wet to absorb a penetrating sealer. The product sits on top, dries unevenly and washes off quickly, and the buyer blames the brand. Wait until the wood passes the water test, where a sprinkle soaks in within a few minutes, before applying any repellent.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the cleaning step</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Applying a repellent over dirt, mildew and loose gray fibers means it bonds to the grime instead of the wood. It looks fine for a few weeks, then wears off along with the dirt. Clean with a deck cleaner, rinse thoroughly, and let the boards dry for a day or two before sealing.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Putting on a thick coat</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Over-applying a penetrating repellent does not add years. The wood can only absorb so much, and the excess dries on the surface as a shiny, sticky or blotchy film that attracts dirt. Apply one even coat at the label's coverage rate and back-brush any puddles so it soaks in.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Never recoating</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A clear repellent is a maintenance product. Applying it once and forgetting it means the deck is effectively unprotected by the second summer. Run the water test each spring, and recoat the sunny boards and traffic lanes as soon as water stops beading. Regular light coats keep the job quick and cheap.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you put Thompson's Water Seal over an old stain?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually only if the old finish has worn away enough for the wood to absorb the new product. A penetrating water repellent cannot soak through an intact film-forming stain, so it will sit on top and may peel or stay tacky. Do the water test first. If water beads on the old finish, the new product has nowhere to go.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is Thompson's Water Seal good for pressure-treated wood?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It can be, once the wood is dry enough to absorb it. Pressure treatment protects against rot and insects but not against water absorption, cracking or graying, so a water repellent helps. Wait until the lumber passes the water test. Many owners use a clear repellent the first year, then move to a tinted product later.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does Thompson's Water Seal make a deck slippery?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A properly applied penetrating water repellent should not leave a slick film because it soaks into the wood. Problems usually come from over-application, where excess product dries on the surface, or from applying it to wood that could not absorb it. Wipe up puddles, apply at the label rate and be careful when the deck is wet.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you use Thompson's Water Seal on a fence?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, and fences are one of the jobs a clear water repellent suits best. Vertical boards shed water and get less direct sun than a deck floor, so the finish tends to last longer. Spraying is usually fastest on fence boards. Protect plants and nearby surfaces from overspray, and avoid windy days.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is Thompson's oil-based or water-based?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Both exist. The brand has sold water-based and oil-based formulas over the years, and the line changes, so the only reliable answer is the label on the specific can. The base affects cleanup, dry time and what the product can go over. Avoid mixing very different products without checking compatibility on the label first.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should you pressure wash before applying Thompson's?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Cleaning before sealing is essential, but a pressure washer is optional and needs care. A deck cleaner and a stiff brush or a low-pressure rinse is often enough. If you do pressure wash, use a fan tip, keep the wand moving and hold it at a distance so you do not raise fibers. Let the wood dry fully afterward.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does Thompson's Water Seal prevent mildew?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It can help by keeping the wood drier, since mildew grows more readily on damp surfaces, and some formulas include mildew-resistant ingredients listed on the label. But it does not stop mildew on its own in shaded, humid areas. Clean the deck before sealing to remove existing growth, and wash it periodically to keep spores from taking hold.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

