        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Thompson's WaterSeal works at what a clear water repellent is meant to do: it makes wood shed water and slows the swelling, shrinking and cracking that rain causes. It does not stop sunlight from graying the wood, and on a sunny deck floor the clear versions typically need a fresh coat about once a year. The tinted and stain versions in the line add pigment and last longer.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Buy the clear product for fences, vertical surfaces, shaded decks and budget annual maintenance. For a sunny deck where you want to keep the color and recoat less often, choose a toner, a semi-transparent sealer-stain or a quality penetrating oil instead. Whatever you pick, clean first, let the wood dry, apply one thin coat and run the water test every spring.
          </p>
